<h1>AI Tools for Video Editing & Filmmaking (2026): What Actually Works vs Marketing Hype</h1>

<p>I'll say it upfront: <strong>AI won't replace editors. But editors who use AI smartly will replace those who don't.</strong> After testing every "revolutionary AI tool" that launched in the last 2 years, here's my honest breakdown of what's genuinely useful, what's overhyped, and what I actually use on real paid projects.</p>

<div class="callout">
<strong>Ajay's honest take:</strong> 90% of "AI video editing tools" are marketing noise. The ones that actually matter are built into software you already use — DaVinci Resolve's Neural Engine, Premiere's auto-transcription, and a few standalone tools like Topaz for upscaling. Everything else is either too expensive, too unreliable, or produces results that look "AI" (and your clients will notice).
</div>

<h2>1. AI Inside DaVinci Resolve — The Tools I Actually Use Daily</h2>

<p>Blackmagic has been quietly building some of the best AI tools in the industry, and they're free in Resolve's standard edition. Here's what works:</p>

<h3>Magic Mask (⭐ Game-Changer)</h3>
<p>Select a person, face, or object and Resolve tracks it across the entire clip automatically. I use this on every single project for:</p>
<ul>
<li>Isolating skin tones for selective grading without manually drawing Power Windows</li>
<li>Separating foreground subject from background for independent exposure/color adjustments</li>
<li>Quick subject relighting without rotoscoping</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Verdict:</strong> This alone is worth upgrading to Resolve Studio (₹23,000 one-time). It saves me 2-3 hours per project compared to manual masking.</p>

<h3>AI Voice Isolation (⭐ Essential for Wedding/Event Work)</h3>
<p>Strips background noise and isolates the human voice. I've used this on wedding vow recordings where the DJ's bass was bleeding into the lapel mic. It's not perfect — heavy bass still bleeds through — but for dialogue cleanup, it's remarkable. This needs Resolve Studio.</p>

<h3>DaVinci Neural Engine Speed Warp</h3>
<p>Creates smooth slow-motion by generating intermediate frames using AI. Shoot at 60fps, slow to 24fps, and Speed Warp fills in the gaps. Results: very good for human subjects, occasionally weird for fast-moving objects with complex edges (spinning bike wheels, flowing water get artifacts).</p>

<h3>Super Scale (Upscaling)</h3>
<p>Upscales 1080p to 4K or 4K to 8K using neural networks. I've tested it on old wedding footage shot at 1080p — the results are noticeably better than standard bicubic scaling, but don't expect miracles. It can't invent detail that isn't there.</p>

<h3>AI Relight (Resolve 21)</h3>
<p>Adds a virtual 3D light source to any clip in post. Sounds amazing on paper. In practice: works well for subtle fill light on faces, but the 3D depth estimation breaks down on complex scenes. I use it for minor corrections, not dramatic relighting.</p>

<h2>2. AI Color Grading — Does It Work?</h2>

<table>
<tr><th>Tool</th><th>What It Does</th><th>Price</th><th>My Verdict</th></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Resolve Auto Color</strong></td><td>One-click color balance using AI scene analysis</td><td>Free</td><td>Good starting point for beginners. I never ship auto-color results — always fine-tune manually.</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Colourlab AI</strong></td><td>AI-powered color matching and look creation</td><td>$299/yr</td><td>Impressive tech, but expensive for Indian freelancers. Better for commercial studios doing high-volume work.</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>LUT-based "AI" plugins</strong></td><td>Apply pre-made looks and call them "AI"</td><td>Varies</td><td>Marketing buzzword abuse. These are just LUT packs with a fancy installer. Skip.</td></tr>
</table>

<p><strong>My honest opinion on AI color grading:</strong> Color grading is the one area where AI genuinely cannot replace human judgment. A machine can match exposure and white balance (correction), but the <em>creative</em> grade — the mood, the emotional tone, the client's "feel" — that requires understanding the story. Every time I've tried to let AI handle a full grade, the client has asked me to redo it. Learn to grade manually first. Use AI to speed up the technical corrections.</p>

