</table>

<h2>4. AI Auto-Captions — The Biggest Time Saver</h2>

<p>This is the one AI feature that has genuinely changed my workflow. Auto-generated captions/subtitles used to take hours. Now it takes minutes.</p>

<table>
<tr><th>Tool</th><th>Accuracy</th><th>Hindi Support</th><th>Price</th><th>My Rating</th></tr>
<tr><td><strong>CapCut Auto Captions</strong></td><td>Very good (English), decent (Hindi)</td><td>Yes (Hinglish works OK)</td><td>Free</td><td>⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Premiere Pro Auto Transcribe</strong></td><td>Excellent (English), improving (Hindi)</td><td>Limited</td><td>CC Subscription</td><td>⭐⭐⭐⭐</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>OpenAI Whisper (open-source)</strong></td><td>Excellent (multi-language)</td><td>Yes</td><td>Free (needs setup)</td><td>⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Descript</strong></td><td>Excellent (English)</td><td>Limited</td><td>$24/month</td><td>⭐⭐⭐⭐</td></tr>
</table>

<p><strong>What I actually do:</strong> For Hindi/Hinglish YouTube content, I use CapCut's auto-captions (free, good accuracy). For English corporate work, Premiere's built-in transcription is excellent. If you need maximum accuracy for any language, run your audio through <a href="/trending-github-repos/whisper">OpenAI Whisper</a> — it's free, open-source, and the best speech-to-text model that exists.</p>

<h2>5. AI Upscaling — When You Need to Save Old Footage</h2>

<table>
<tr><th>Tool</th><th>Best For</th><th>Price</th><th>Speed</th></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Topaz Video AI</strong></td><td>Best overall quality, 1080p→4K upscaling</td><td>$299 one-time</td><td>Slow (GPU-dependent)</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>DaVinci Super Scale</strong></td><td>Good quality, built into Resolve timeline</td><td>Studio only</td><td>Real-time on strong GPU</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>FFmpeg + RealESRGAN</strong></td><td>Free alternative, command-line</td><td>Free</td><td>Very slow</td></tr>
</table>

<p>I used Topaz Video AI to upscale a client's 720p wedding footage from 2018 to 4K for a anniversary compilation. The result was impressive — not native 4K quality, but genuinely watchable on a big screen. Worth the ₹25,000 if you do archival/restoration work. For most editors, DaVinci Super Scale is good enough and already built into your workflow.</p>

<h2>6. AI Music & Sound Generation — Proceed with Caution</h2>

<p>Tools like Suno and Udio can generate surprisingly good music from text prompts. But:</p>

<ul>
<li><strong>Copyright is murky.</strong> Nobody has definitively settled whether AI-generated music is copyright-free for commercial use. Using it in client work is risky.</li>
<li><strong>It sounds "AI."</strong> Listeners are getting better at spotting generated music. The uncanny valley is real.</li>
<li><strong>Better alternative:</strong> Use royalty-free music libraries (Epidemic Sound, Artlist, or even YouTube Audio Library for free). The cost is predictable and the legal risk is zero.</li>
</ul>

<h2>7. What AI Still Cannot Do (And Won't for Years)</h2>

<ul>
<li><strong>Understand story.</strong> AI can cut on beats and match transitions, but it has no sense of narrative pacing, emotional arc, or dramatic tension.</li>
<li><strong>Read clients.</strong> Half of editing is understanding what the client <em>actually</em> wants vs what they said they wanted. That's a human skill.</li>
<li><strong>Make creative decisions.</strong> Should this shot be warm or cool? Should the edit breathe here or cut fast? These are emotional, subjective judgments that AI handles poorly.</li>
<li><strong>Handle Indian content nuances.</strong> Bollywood-style pacing, regional music sync, cultural references in wedding videos — AI doesn't get these.</li>
</ul>

<h2>FAQ</h2>

<details><summary>Will AI replace video editors by 2030?</summary>
<p>No. AI will automate the boring parts (transcription, rough cuts, basic color correction, format conversion). The creative, client-facing, storytelling parts of editing require human judgment. What will happen: editors who refuse to learn AI tools will lose work to editors who use them to work faster. The skill set is evolving, not dying.</p>
</details>

<details><summary>Should I still learn manual color grading if AI can do it?</summary>
<p>Absolutely yes. AI auto-color is a starting point, not a finishing point. Every professional colorist I know — including myself — fine-tunes everything manually. AI gets you 60% of the way. The last 40% is where the craft lives, and that's what clients pay for. Learn the fundamentals with our <a href="/davinci-resolve-nodes-explained-2026">nodes guide</a> and <a href="/davinci-resolve-color-management-cst-vs-aces">color management workflow</a>.</p>
</details>

<details><summary>What's the best free AI tool for video editing?</summary>
<p>DaVinci Resolve's free version includes basic Magic Mask and Speed Warp. CapCut (free) has excellent auto-captions. OpenAI Whisper (free, open-source) is the best speech-to-text for any language. These three together cover 80% of what you need.</p>
</details>

<details><summary>Is AI-generated music safe to use in client projects?</summary>
<p>I wouldn't risk it for commercial work until copyright law catches up. Use Epidemic Sound (₹1,000/month for Indian creators), YouTube Audio Library (free), or Artlist instead. The legal certainty is worth the cost.</p>
</details>

